# ===========================================================================
# BOX:      the PUBLIC container (fresh unprivileged CPU-only LXC, "vidtheque")
# INSTALLS: BEFORE-SHIP.md Phase 4.1 — "Write stack.env from .env.example"
# GOES TO:  $VIDTHEQUE_DATA_DIR/stack.env  (= /var/lib/vidtheque/stack.env)
# READ BY:  vidtheque-mcp.service, via EnvironmentFile=
#
# STAGED, NOT INSTALLED. Nothing here runs until the container exists and this
# file is copied into place. Placeholders are `<LIKE_THIS>` and every one of
# them is listed in deploy/staging/install.md's "fill these in" table.
#
# Provenance: research/release-staging-2026-08-11.md §5.4 (the template),
# BEFORE-SHIP.md Phase 1 (the nine answered decisions, 2026-08-11 ~11:45),
# deploy/.env.example (the document of record — every key below has an entry
# there, which is the CLAUDE.md rule and the §2.4 key-set diff).
#
# THIS FILE IS NOT A COPY OF THE SANDBOX'S stack.env AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE
# (§5.4). The sandbox's is a development configuration; copying it is the
# single easiest way to ship the wrong mode.
#
# BOOLEANS ARE STRICT SINCE THE G1 MERGE (commit a4e3027): every flag must read
# 1/true/yes/on or 0/false/no/off. `Y`, `2`, `enabled` are now a BOOT FAILURE
# by design. Case and surrounding space are fine. Grep this file before first
# boot — BEFORE-SHIP.md G1, last checked item.
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mode — the intended production combination (docs/design/demo-site.md §1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# No credential: the public instance is a real MCP server anyone can add to
# their own agent. That is the point of the demo.
VIDTHEQUE_AUTH=none

# Demo mode: write tools and write routes are never registered (404, not 403),
# and the dashboard's projection is redacted. Phase 4.3 proves this reached the
# process by asking /healthz for writes_allowed: false — reading this file is
# not the check.
VIDTHEQUE_PUBLIC_READONLY=1

# Explicit 0 rather than absent. This is the one flag that would let AUTH=none
# register the write tools on a public hostname; without it that combination
# refuses to boot, which is exactly the guard we want standing. It is not a
# thing to leave on, and on this box it is not a thing to turn on at all —
# indexing does not resume while public (Phase 1 decision 8).
VIDTHEQUE_ALLOW_PUBLIC_WRITES=0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hostname — two variables, two silent disasters (deploy-public.md §3)
#
# Hostname decided 2026-08-11 (Phase 1 decision 1): vidtheque.dev, bought this
# morning, moving to Cloudflare nameservers. The zone must read **Active**
# before `cloudflared tunnel route dns` will accept it (Phase 5).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Full origin, scheme included, https even though cloudflared reaches the
# origin over plain http on loopback: it describes what the world sees. Every
# absolute URL is built from it and none is derived from the request — get it
# wrong and /api/meta hands every visitor an mcp_url pointing at their own
# laptop, and every thumbnail on the demo page 404s.
PUBLIC_URL=https://vidtheque.dev

# Bare host. No scheme, no port. This one feeds the MCP transport's
# DNS-rebinding guard: wrong, and /mcp answers 421 to everything while `/`,
# /api/*, /frames/*, /dashboard/* and /healthz all work perfectly. Nothing
# validates it at boot under AUTH=none. Phase 6 checks /mcp FIRST for this
# reason.
VIDTHEQUE_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME=vidtheque.dev

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The bind address — codex review blocker #1 (§4.4, §10.1)
#
# These two lines are the ones the original plan forgot. mcp/__main__.py:22-24
# defaults to 0.0.0.0:8080, and scripts/dev_stack.sh only ever passed them
# INLINE, so they have never lived in a stack.env before. A systemd unit that
# just reads this file therefore binds 0.0.0.0:8080: the tunnel 502s against
# 127.0.0.1:8100, AND the origin becomes LAN-reachable — where a forged
# CF-Connecting-IP mints a fresh bucket per request and voids every per-IP
# limit (deploy-public.md §4).
#
# They cannot be pinned in the unit instead: measured on systemd 257 (Debian
# 13, the target), `EnvironmentFile=` overrides `Environment=` regardless of
# the order the directives appear in. So this file is the single source of
# truth, and vidtheque-mcp.service carries ExecStartPre guards that refuse to
# start if either line is missing or changed. Do not delete them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

VIDTHEQUE_HOST=127.0.0.1
VIDTHEQUE_PORT=8100

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client IP behind the tunnel (deploy-public.md §4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Keep the default. Behind the tunnel cloudflared connects from loopback, so
# every request has the same socket address; emptying this would put the whole
# internet in one 30/min search bucket. Trusting it is earned by the line
# above: the origin binds loopback and the tunnel is the only way in.
VIDTHEQUE_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER=CF-Connecting-IP

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dashboard — PUBLIC with redactions (Phase 1 decision 4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 1 = /dashboard is served. With VIDTHEQUE_PUBLIC_READONLY=1 the jobs view
# keeps states, codes, counts and clocks and drops source URLs, error text and
# event messages; the overview drops model ids, byte totals and the auth line.
# There is therefore NO `^/dashboard -> http_status:404` ingress rule in
# cloudflared-config.yml — that block stays commented, deliberately.
# Phase 4.3 and Phase 6 both re-run the deploy-public.md §2.5 redaction greps;
# the second run, through the tunnel, is the one that counts.
VIDTHEQUE_DASHBOARD=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GATE G2a — this line MUST stay empty, and empty means no value at all
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This allowlist grants OWNER status on the SOCKET PEER address. Behind a
# tunnel the socket peer is cloudflared, so any CIDR covering loopback or the
# container network makes every anonymous visitor an owner: owner clamps on
# both API prefixes and the max_text_chars=0 full-transcript hatch that
# demo-site.md §2 reserves for an owner's agent.
#
# Since Tom's 2026-08-11 decision (commit 2f29bdd) the code REFUSES TO BOOT on
# exactly this combination: a trusted CIDR overlapping loopback/RFC1918/docker,
# AND VIDTHEQUE_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER set (it is, above), AND a non-loopback
# VIDTHEQUE_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME (it is, above). All three are true on this box, so
# putting anything on the right of the `=` here is a boot failure, not a
# warning. That is the intended behaviour — a warning is a control that depends
# on someone reading it.
#
# The G2a grep stays in the checklist anyway, because a check that runs is
# worth more than a guarantee you have to reason about:
#   grep -E '^VIDTHEQUE_DASHBOARD_TRUSTED_CIDRS=.+' $DATA/stack.env   # NOTHING
VIDTHEQUE_DASHBOARD_TRUSTED_CIDRS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The private worker link — topology B's one new rule (§4.5)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The worker stays on the sandbox box and is reached over the host bridge on a
# private address. It answers an UNAUTHENTICATED OpenAI-compatible API — the
# firewall is the entire authorization story, and it must accept :8081 only
# from this container's address (Phase 4.2).
#
# A public search calls this URL twice, sequentially, on a 20 s timeout each,
# so a dead worker costs ~40 s and then silently returns FTS-only results with
# a note:. Phase 4.3's leg check is the proof it works, and it must be run
# THROUGH MCP — /api/search does not carry leg_counts.
# CHANGED (2026-08-11, Topology A): the worker runs ON THIS BOX, loopback.
# The unauthenticated inference API never touches any network interface.
WORKER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The worker (same box, same env file — Topology A, decided in the field)
# vidtheque-worker.service reads this file too. VIDTHEQUE_HOST=127.0.0.1 above
# is the bind for BOTH processes; the ports differ by variable name.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VIDTHEQUE_WORKER_PORT=8081
DEVICE=cuda
EMBED_RESIDENT=0
EMBED_BACKEND=qwen3-vl-embedding
IMAGE_EMBED_BACKEND=qwen3-vl-embedding
EMBED_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B
IMAGE_EMBED_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3-VL-Embedding-2B
EMBED_DIM=0
IDLE_UNLOAD_SECONDS=3600
VIDTHEQUE_STT_POLICY=prefer_whisperx
VIDTHEQUE_KEYFRAME_EXTRACT_WORKERS=4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Proxmox VOLUME mount point (mp0, backup=1) — never a bind mount: bind
# mounts are never included in vzdump, which turns "the container is backed up"
# into "everything except the corpus is backed up" (§4.1).
VIDTHEQUE_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/vidtheque

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ask mode — ON day one (Phase 1 decision 2)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Tom's EXISTING spend-capped OpenRouter key. NEVER commit the value; this file
# is staged in the repo, so it carries a placeholder and the real key is pasted
# in on the box, once, at Phase 4.1. The spend cap at OpenRouter is the control
# that actually bounds the money — everything below only bounds the volume.
#
# If this is left as the placeholder the mode is not "off", it is "broken": an
# empty value means off (/api/meta reports ask_enabled: false and the page
# hides the toggle), but a non-empty junk value means the button is offered and
# 503s. Either paste the key or empty the line.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=PASTE_THE_CAPPED_KEY_HERE

# Server-wide, per UTC day, PERSISTED to the ask_budget table (migration 0005),
# so a restart resumes the day's count instead of handing the cap back. Left at
# the shipped default: 50 answers x up to 4 tool rounds of a paid model. It is
# a volume guard, not a spend cap — the spend cap is on the key.
VIDTHEQUE_RATE_ASK_PER_DAY=50

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limits
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# /mcp per IP per minute. NOTE — the runbook's "/mcp is deliberately never rate
# limited" is now STALE: the G1 security merge added this bucket (2026-08-10
# audit F-1) and .env.example ships it at 120. It is left at the default here.
#
# The Cloudflare edge rule (Phase 1 decision 9, ~60 req/min/IP) therefore sits
# IN FRONT of a limit that is already there, and being tighter it is the one
# that will fire. Both are wanted: the edge rule is the brake that works before
# a request reaches the box, and this one is the brake that still works if
# somebody ever reaches the origin another way. See
# deploy/staging/cloudflare-dashboard-checklist.md §2.
VIDTHEQUE_RATE_MCP_PER_MIN=120

# search 30/min, ask 5/min, frames 120/min, dashboard 120/min are left at their
# shipped defaults and are therefore absent from this file on purpose — a key
# in .env.example that is not here is fine; a key here that is not in
# .env.example is a bug (deploy-public.md §2.4).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Misc
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

LOG_LEVEL=info

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS FILE — read this list, it is half the point
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# IDLE_UNLOAD_SECONDS / EMBED_RESIDENT / EMBED_* / IMAGE_EMBED_* / DEVICE /
# STT_* / OCR_* / GPU_*  — these are read by worker/, and the worker is on the
#   OTHER box. Setting them here changes nothing at all, and Phase 1's latency
#   decision would then silently never happen while the Phase 6 cold-search
#   measurement cheerfully confirms the old behaviour (§5.4, review finding 11).
#   They live in deploy/staging/stack.env.sandbox.
#
# VIDTHEQUE_YTDLP_* / VIDTHEQUE_RATE_LIMIT_BACKOFF_S / VIDTHEQUE_STT_POLICY /
# VIDTHEQUE_KEYFRAME_* — indexing knobs. This box does not index. The job
#   runner unfortunately still STARTS here (app.py:82,191 — run_pipeline
#   defaults True and is not tied to public mode), which is why Phase 2.1
#   drains and verifies the queue terminally before the snapshot: a database
#   migrated with live queue rows has the PUBLIC box fetching from YouTube
#   behind the tunnel (§5.2b).
#
# TUNNEL_TOKEN — that is the remotely-managed tunnel flow. We run locally
#   managed, so ingress is a file in this repo you can grep and diff
#   (§6.1). Leave it unset and leave the Zero Trust dashboard's ingress empty
#   for this tunnel; mixing the two is a documented source of 502s.
#
# VIDTHEQUE_SECRET / secret.key — the new box mints its own (§5.4, Phase 3.7).
#   Under AUTH=none nothing signs anything and no live credential depends on it.
#
# VIDTHEQUE_DERIVED_CACHE_MB — left at the shipped 256 MB. deploy-public.md
#   §2.6's remeasured arithmetic says the five product widths across the frozen
#   corpus are ~1.6 GB, i.e. ~6.4x over that cap, so the LRU is permanently hot
#   under any crawl and the 192px thumbnails are the ones that survive. That is
#   a CPU/latency decision, not a correctness one, and the Cloudflare edge
#   absorbs most of it. Raise it on day one only if `du -sh $DATA/derived` and
#   the disk say so — see install.md's post-install notes.
